---
/* Our line, drawn.

   The smallest chart in the estate, and the one where that is the point. Eight
   people. Two of the four grandparent branches stop at the first generation
   above the line, because Argentine civil registration is not open and the
   1908 enrolment card names Juan Carlos and Maria and then says nothing more.

   The Taylor side goes one generation further than people.json alone would
   give, because this archive already wrote that link down: taylor-line.json
   has James Taylor as William's father in plain text. Reading a link the
   archive has already made is not a new claim.

   Anton and his sisters are living and are not drawn. The chart begins with
   Nuno, born 1924, which is where this archive begins naming people freely. */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import AncChart from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/AncChart.astro";
import { lineage } from "@daviddef/archive-kit/lib/ahnen.js";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import people from "../data/people.json";
import { countryOf, COUNTRIES } from "../lib/country.js";
import taylor from "../data/taylor-line.json";

const bySlug = new Map(people.map((p) => [p.slug, p]));

/* The Taylor chain is written oldest-first with letter generations. Only the
   two rows above Mary Septima are wanted here — the rest are her sisters. */
const james = taylor.find((t) => t.gen === "A");

/* A parent is either a person this archive has a record for, or a name on a
   document and nothing else. Both belong on the chart; only the first gets a
   link. */
const asRow = (o) => {
  if (!o) return null;
  if (o.slug && bySlug.has(o.slug)) return bySlug.get(o.slug);
  return o.name ? { name: o.name, born: o.dates || "", _named: true, via: o.via } : null;
};

const parentsOf = (r) => {
  /* The one link this archive holds outside people.json. */
  if (r._named && james && /^William Taylor$/i.test(String(r.name).trim()))
    return { f: { name: james.person, born: james.born, _named: true, via: "tree" }, m: null };
  return { f: asRow(r.father), m: asRow(r.mother) };
};

const yearOf = (v) => (String(v ?? "").match(/\d{4}/) || [""])[0];

const root = lineage(bySlug.get("nuno-fernando-lerena"), {
  parentsOf,
  born: (r) => yearOf(r.born),
  died: (r) => yearOf(r.died),
  /* AncChart renders a place as PLAIN TEXT, so the country cannot be a coloured pill
     here as it is on the home-page tree and the spine - the component would have to
     accept markup, and it is shared with two other archives. The country is appended
     to the string instead, which puts the same information on the chart without
     forking the kit. Skipped when the place already names its country, so Uruguay
     does not come out as "Uruguay, Uruguay". */
  place: (r) => {
    const p = r.birthplace || r.place || "";
    const c = countryOf(p);
    if (!c) return p;
    const name = COUNTRIES[c];
    return p.toLowerCase().includes(name.toLowerCase()) ? p : `${p} \u00b7 ${name}`;
  },
  living: (r) => false,
  /* Read in a register, or taken from the family tree. The `via` a parent was
     reached by says which, and an inline parent has no record of its own. */
  conf: (r) => (r._named ? (r.via === "register" ? "doc" : "lore")
                         : (r.source ? "doc" : "lore")),
  extra: (r) => ({ slug: r._named ? null : r.slug }),
});

const personHref = (b) => (b.slug ? u(`/people/${b.slug}/`) : null);

let drawn = 0, doc = 0, walls = 0, deepest = 0;
(function count(x, g = 0) {
  if (!x) return;
  if (x.wall) { walls++; return; }
  drawn++; if (x.conf === "doc") doc++;
  if (g > deepest) deepest = g;
  count(x.f, g + 1); count(x.m, g + 1);
})(root);
---
<Base title="Our line, drawn"
      eyebrow={`${drawn} people, ${walls} walls`}
      dek="The smallest chart in the estate, and the shortest answer to how far this line goes back: eight people, and then the doors close.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="prose">This is the whole of it: <strong>{drawn}</strong> people,
    <strong>{deepest + 1}</strong> generations at the deepest point, and <strong>{walls}</strong>
    branches that stop dead — and the stopping is the finding. Argentine civil registration is not open to the public, so the line runs into a
    wall the moment it crosses back over the Atlantic.</p>

    <p class="prose">What holds the Argentine side up at all is one document: the 1908
    <a href={u("/enrolment-card/")}>ficha de enrolamiento</a>, which names Pablo's father and
    mother and then stops. Everything above them would need a register this archive cannot
    reach from here.</p>

    <p class="prose">The English side goes further on paper — <a href={u("/taylor/")}>the
    Taylors</a> reach James, born around 1796 — and that is the archive's own reading of its
    own file rather than a new claim. The seven Taylor daughters who are not in this line are
    on that page, not this one.</p>
  </section>

  <AncChart root={root} personHref={personHref} />

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="prose dim">Each box names the country after the place, in <strong>today&rsquo;s
    borders</strong> &mdash; an anachronism this archive would rather admit than hide, since
    Canelones in 1791 was the <em>Banda Oriental</em> of the Spanish empire. Where a place
    cannot be assigned it is left alone: bare <em>Rosario</em> is Santa Fe in Argentina or
    Colonia in Uruguay and this register holds both, and bare <em>C&oacute;rdoba</em> is the
    open question of whether Sancho came from Andalusia or Argentina.</p>

    <p class="prose dim">The living are not drawn. The chart starts at Nuno Fernando Lerena,
    born 1924. What it would take to get past each of these walls is on
    <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open Questions</a>; the lines this family marries into are
    on <a href={u("/trees/")}>the trees</a>.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
